UCB leverages Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs), where each weight parameter is represented by a probability distribution (mean and variance) approximated via variational inference using the Bayes-by-Backprop (BBB) framework. For UCB, the learning rate of each parameter's mean (
	µ
) is inversely scaled by its uncertainty (standard deviation, 
	σ
) after each task (
	Ωµ = 1/σ, Ωρ = 1
), allowing less uncertain (more important) parameters to change less. For UCB-P, a hard-threshold variant, importance is measured by the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (
	SNR = |µ|/σ
). Parameters with low SNR are pruned (set to zero) and marked with task-specific binary masks, freezing important parameters for subsequent tasks. Both methods use stochastic gradient descent and operate in a task-incremental learning setting without relying on episodic memory or accessing past task data.

